    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for that. regarding the undervalued assets, if it looks like they were just missed due to poor record keeping rather than being intentionally obscured, does HMRC treat that differently? also, how do they typically go about finding these \"hidden\" amounts if they aren't disclosed upfront?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "HMRC's approach to undervalued assets hinges on intent, though the difference between \"poor record keeping\" and \"intentional obscuring\" can be a fine line, as they often look at the overall picture of the deceased's affairs. If it's genuinely a clerical error, demonstrating that good faith effort was made to locate everything can mitigate penalties, but you still owe the tax on the value. They don't just wait for you to find them; HMRC has extensive powers to investigate, which can involve tracing bank accounts, reviewing property deeds, and cross-referencing financial data.\n\nIt's very wise to be proactive now, rather than waiting for HMRC to come knocking with their own investigative methods. They are very thorough, and sometimes their pursuit of missing funds is more relentless than a family's search for a lost piece of the family's history. I highly recommend consulting a specialist who can help structure the disclosure in the most defensible way possible.",
      "source": "target",
